ry—even with this fine-grained scale, I'm only seeing a 0.6-point difference (p=0.55, Cohen's d=0.04), which means the formality effect is too weak to detect reliably. This suggests there's an attenuation ceiling where even increased scale sensitivity can't overcome a genuinely small effect.\n\nWait, I need to clarify something about the experimental structure here. The 100-point Likert results are part of the ablation analysis mentioned in section C4, not the main exp-003 design which focuses on comparing Likert versus pairwise sensitivity across multiple judges.\n\nLet me check the experiment plan more carefully—exp-003 is specifically about computing both Likert ATE and pairwise win rates on the same counterfactual data to systematically compare measurement sensitivity. The fine-grained Likert ablation would naturally fit as part of this sensitivity analysis.\n\nI should integrate these 100-point Likert findings into exp-003 and then write out the complete YAML for all planned experiments, being careful to distinguish between what's already been executed versus what's still pending.\n\nLooking at the experiment plan, I need to include every experiment marked as planned, even if some have already been completed in the registry—I'll note which ones are done and generate worst-case predictions for the rest.\n\nFor experiments that are already finished, I'll show data consistent with what actually happened since those outcomes are fixed. For the truly planned ones, I'll create imagined worst-case scenarios. Now I'm going to write out the complete YAML with all the experiments organized and detailed.\n\nLooking at exp-021 with Llama-3.3-70B as the key worst-case scenario: the model shows no meaningful bias on either formality or verbosity axes, matching Claude's pattern. The formality results show near-neutral preference (52% for formal with p=0.55), high tie rates around 68%, and minimal effect sizes, while verbosity similarly shows weak preferences and consistency r