nterfactual quality is stable regardless of which rewriter generates the examples.\n\nNow I'm examining the next experiment on GPT-4o's Likert scoring for verbosity and register, where I already have pairwise results but need to complete the average treatment effect analysis for the Likert ratings. Now I'm reviewing the GEE odds ratios and confirming that the verbosity and register findings hold up across different statistical approaches—the Likert scale effects remain consistent even under worst-case scenarios, though the 100-point scale detection shows some variation in effect size. GPT-4o shows blindspots across all three axes—formality, verbosity, and register—with significant binomial tests but non-significant Likert results, mirroring the pattern I saw with Qwen3-32B and suggesting a consistent cross-axis blindspot among biased judges. Now I'm setting up the Llama-3.3-70B experiment as a worst-case scenario where this model shows no bias on either formality or verbosity axes, with detailed consistency rates and statistical parameters already mapped out. Now I'm looking at the verbosity metrics, which show similar patterns to formality—the Bradley-Terry model gives a point estimate around 0.538 with a confidence interval spanning 0.462 to 0.614, and the p-value of 0.35 suggests no significant effect. The Likert and GEE analyses are also non-significant across the board, with odds ratios close to 1 and high p-values, indicating minimal differences in how the model handles verbose versus concise instructions. The Llama results show performance in the 52-54% range for both axes, which aligns with Claude's pattern where only originality shows significant effect on formality while style has minimal impact, and verbosity shows no meaningful effects. This suggests Llama's training prioritized style neutrality similarly to Claude, unlike GPT-4o and Qwen3-32B which appear to have different style preferences baked in.\n\nThe TOST equivalence test confirms Llama's lack of